      "math_academy_id": "e-18929",
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+1}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+1}-1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} &= \\[5pt]\n                \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1})^2-1^2} &= \\[5pt]\n                \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x+1-1} &= \\[5pt]\n                \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x}&=\\[5pt]\n\\sqrt{x+1}-1\n\t}\n$$",
